WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:08.530
We move now to something perhaps not quite
in the same uplifting league, but it is a

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.100
report from Bill McCarthy on the establishment
programme closedown.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:19.539
Preamble
Bill McCarthy: You will recall, during the

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:27.570
course of last year the heart of what we were
doing was establishing ourselves in the commissioning

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:33.379
system, and we identified 13 critical success
factors which we tracked ourselves on.

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:39.310
We very openly tracked ourselves on the progress
of those with the attendant risks that we

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:45.840
were managing and putting in place a contingency
where we needed to.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:53.070
We shall be drawing that together into a formal
closedown report so that all of the strands

00:00:53.070 --> 00:01:00.820
of that work can be followed through and can
be located, and we will do that publically.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:07.830
I thought that rather than wait for that full
report the Board would appreciate just a flavour

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:17.160
of, particularly, how this first month in
our operations, which is a real test of our

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:26.149
success in achieving those factors which we
set out, because this is what this brief verbal

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:28.079
update is about.

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:35.600
A lot of what we are focusing on was around
our workforce, recruiting, developing our

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.560
people -- and I think Jo is going to cover
that in the subsequent report -- and also

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:47.689
Victor's report of the authorisation subcommittee
will tell us about CCG establishment.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.820
I will focus more on ourselves.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.360
Work streams
We have 49 work streams that we were following

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.060
through.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.409
Of those we have successfully closed 25.

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:12.010
So 25 of those were just about transition
and we got them to like a green state where

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:21.970
we were comfortable and those now are part
of building the organisation. 19 of them are

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:28.480
part of our ongoing business now, so they
were really important things that we needed

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.830
to do.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:35.190
For example, setting up our organisational
development work, but they do not come to

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:43.000
an end with the transition period; they are
here with us and so they are incorporated

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.230
in the business plan.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:56.730
There are just five which we thought were
critical to our establishment but which we

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:01.880
are still going to be working on over a period
of about six months.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:39.230
Those are things like our corporate 

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.180
IT systems.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:54.159
I 
would refer back to your opening remarks,

00:03:54.159 --> 00:04:02.420
Chairman, about just the scale and complexity
of the task at hand, which has been immense.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.800
Word of thanks
I am just exceptionally grateful to the teams,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.930
a small band of -- I was going to say volunteers
--actually most of them were volunteers.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:19.480
Most of them came on to join us when we were
the Commissioning Board Authority and did

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.900
it without certainty of having a future role
with us.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:30.320
They all had to go through the recruitment
processes and the energy, expertise, commitment

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.870
that those people put in to help us get to
this point I think is quite extra ordinary,

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:40.560
so I would like to thank them really seriously.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:48.040
I would also like to thank all of those people
who have joined us in the past few weeks because,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:58.060
by its nature, we had to put in a whole series
of workarounds just to get us into a reasonable

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.060
operating position.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:05.830
That has meant we have relied on quite a lot
of cooperation, understanding, tolerance,

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:14.110
and if you wanted the first real time hard
day's test to the values, I think it is in

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:19.210
those behaviours that we have experienced
over the past few weeks, where of course there

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.270
have been problems that have cropped up.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:28.110
The cooperation between people, brand new
-- who could be based in Cornwall or based

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:35.090
in Northumberland -- in understanding the
challenge in working with us, in getting onto

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:41.440
computer support, state support, people support
help lines, and working through the issues

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.170
in a really cooperative way with the right
sort of respect and understanding, I think

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.790
has been great.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:53.290
To them as well, thanks for the tolerance,
bearing with us and now for that full engagement

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:59.610
of making sure that we are not just learning
from experience, but really driving on that

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:05.180
to make this a very professional and excellent
organisation that we are committed to over

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.120
the coming months.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.920
I will capture this very fully in a written
report but I thought you would appreciate

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:11.920
just a flavour of how we are doing, Chairman.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:12.920
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Bill, very much

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.010
for that and the final comments.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:23.240
I think everybody on the Board remembers that
over the past year at each Board meeting,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:29.280
we have received a report on your 39 work
streams, red, amber, green, and which the

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.180
dominant colour was red.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:37.280
That denoted to us a very significant measure
of risk about all that we were doing, and

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:41.160
cumulative risk that it was all happening
all at once.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.880
So we are very reassured by your report this
morning, but also for the promise of a full

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:46.880
report in due course.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:47.880
Ed?

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.990
Ed Smith: One quick point because it sometimes
happens.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:07:02.830
The 19 that were in the transition report
and are now being disaggregated into business-as-usual

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:10.100
-- are we very clear that those to whom it
is being handed have taken the baton and have

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.680
picked it up and there are no gaps?

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.400
Bill McCarthy: Yes, we are.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.930
They will be incorporated now in the directorate
work plans to support delivery of the business

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.710
plan, and we will have a process just to check
that the handover has not just been done but

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.530
that it has been caught as well.

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:34.330
On the half dozen transition elements, I am
getting weekly reporting on those just to

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.600
make sure we are right on top of them.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.190
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Bill,
for that reassurance.

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:46.180
We turn now to the HR report which is actually
something that has been involved, I think,

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.890
in all our discussions this morning.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.390
I will invite Jo to present her paper.

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:51.310
Policy and Implementation
Jo-Ann Wass

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.220
National Director: Human Resources
Preamble

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.860
Thank you, Chairman, and thank you for the
early billing.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:00.360
From an HR perspective I think this meeting
of the Board marks a bit of a turning point

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:05.270
and that has been reflected in people's earlier
comments, a point around which I think we

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:08.040
are pretty glad we have now turned.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:12.530
At virtually every meeting for the last year
or so, as Bill says, we have discussed a report

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:18.310
on HR issues, our recruitment plans, our progress
against our recruitment plans in preparation

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.160
for our go live date of 1st April.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:23.930
We are now live; we have now gone live.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:29.610
We are reaching the end of the setup phase
as far as the HR project is concerned.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:34.500
I want to report to the Board on three areas
today where I think we have seen considerable

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.240
effort across the organisation.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:43.370
Every region, every area team, every directorate
working together to achieve some good results

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:44.550
I think.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:50.420
I want to talk about recruitment, the recruitment
position, offer letters and payroll.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:53.400
Recruitment
The paper that you have in front of you has

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:57.710
a lot of numbers in it and I am going to try
and pull out the key ones.

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:01.460
Chairman, I think you had a slight slip of
the tongue earlier where you transposed two

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.050
figures around.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:12.410
The establishment figure is 6,700; not 7,600,
so we have 6,700 posts in the establishment

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:15.380
and we have filled about 6,000 of those.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:19.370
That is an 89% fill rate.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:27.390
We paid round about 5,600 people and the balance,
the 400 is people that have not started yet

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:30.570
so we do not need to pay yet.

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:34.310
Source of recruits
I know this is of interest to the Board; people

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:36.670
have asked me questions about it before.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.720
The source of our recruits is interesting.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:47.980
When you look at where people have joined
us from about 89% came from the previous NHS

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:54.750
system that was being reformed, so SHAs, PCTs,
Department of Health, ALBs, and about 11%

00:09:54.750 --> 00:10:00.830
came from other places, so other parts of
the NHS that were not being reformed and other

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:01.830
sectors.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:05.050
Fill rate
When you look at the fill rate across the

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.501
organisation it does vary.

00:10:06.501 --> 00:10:11.500
You will that it is almost 100% of posts that
are filled in London, in the London regional

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:17.900
structure, and about 75% of posts in the national
support centre.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:18.900
Recruitment efforts are continuing.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:24.243
There are no areas that have given us particular,
specific concerns, and the only other thing

00:10:24.243 --> 00:10:29.920
I need to point out about recruitment is there
is an error in paragraph 14, I think, of the

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:35.140
report which says that the south has a fill
rate of 84%.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:36.140
That should be 91%.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:40.260
It is correct in the table that is attached;
it just wrong in the narrative.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:44.241
In fact, on 1st April, Midlands and East where
the region with the lowest fill rate; they

00:10:44.241 --> 00:10:49.120
had a fill rate of about 88%, so I apologise
for that.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.360
Offer letters
On offer letters, which we have talked about

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:56.630
before, you will recall at one stage we did
have a bit of a backlog around offer letters

00:10:56.630 --> 00:11:02.590
and I am pleased to report that this has now
been cleared so staff now do have their offer

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.030
letters.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:08.680
For some individuals where their circumstances
are a bit more complicated than others, primarily

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:13.420
I think civil servants joining us from the
Department of Health, we are still handling

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:18.100
queries arising from the offer letters, but
for the majority of staff the terms of their

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:22.460
appointment are now settled and we are into
very small numbers where we are trying to

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:26.990
pick up and resolve queries.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:31.310
The staff who joined us from the existing
system; if you recall, I said 89% of the people

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:39.190
who we have recruited came from the previous
system, they joined us on what is known as

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.190
transfer scheme.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:45.090
It is a statutory instrument that moves the
employment of those staff from one body to

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.090
another.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:51.400
They do not need us to give them an employment
contract because we adopt the employment contract

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:56.680
that they had at the point of transfer; for
those people we do not need to do employment

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:57.680
contracts.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:03.540
For the other 11% of staff we do and we are
mostly doing them as we recruit people but

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:07.590
that is the next big job for the HR team to
make sure everyone who needs one has got a

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.290
contract of employment.

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:13.650
Payroll
The big area that I want to update the Board

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:53.790
is around payroll because at the time of writing
the paper we had not run the first payroll.

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:57.950
If you had chosen a month to do it April would
probably not have been the month chosen to

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:04.031
do it because there were four considerable
changes also happening in April that were

00:13:04.031 --> 00:13:06.750
not within our control.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:14.070
In April people get new PAYE codes, new personal
tax allowances were coming into being, there

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:20.029
was 1% increase in agenda for change pay scales,
and there was an increase in NHS pension scheme

00:13:20.029 --> 00:13:25.210
contributions which were an almost 3% increase
for some staff.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:29.330
That is the second year, by the way, of planned
increases; there is another year to come so

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:34.610
they will go up again next year; so a really
complicated change process.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:37.310
Communications
For those reasons we put a number of mechanisms

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:42.870
in place to help us manage this project.

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:46.730
I think you will have noticed we visibly stepped
up communications to staff in the couple of

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:51.370
months running up to the payroll, so we have
been writing to staff on a weekly basis.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:58.960
We put a help line in place, and as a contingency
we put two further pay runs in after the first

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:03.010
and the main pay run on 26th April.

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:07.870
I am pleased to report that we have paid,
since last Friday which was the main payroll,

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.089
5,656 people.

00:14:10.089 --> 00:14:16.770
95% of them on that first pay run, 2% of them
on the second one, which was Tuesday earlier

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:20.410
this week, and 3% on the third pay run which
is today.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:27.060
For 1% of the overall staff that were paid
were made by faster payments, so same day

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:32.500
payments so you can see we did not need to
make extensive use of the supplementary pay

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:35.920
runs, which was a very good thing.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:37.529
The help line has been busy.

00:14:37.529 --> 00:14:42.080
It is being run for us, by the way, by the
NHS Business Services Authority; it is being

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:43.480
run out of Newcastle.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:49.010
It has been busy I think principally because
of all the complexities around pay changes

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:54.370
and tax changes and pension changes at the
beginning of April, but it has held up reasonably

00:14:54.370 --> 00:15:02.370
well and we have had good feedback about the
responsiveness and the helpful attitude of

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.180
people in Newcastle.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.680
Employee records
Everyone is in now.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:10.000
It feels good to have everyone in.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:16.630
I think people have said the spirit has risen
a little bit and it feels very positive.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:20.560
From an employee record's point of view, for
the next couple of months what we are going

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:24.170
to do now is put right where we need to put
right.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:29.050
Small pay sheets for individuals and we are
going to clean up and organise the data that

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.170
we have in place.

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:36.160
We are going to conduct an intensive data
refresh across all our employee records and

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:40.140
check with every individual employee that
we have the right information about them.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:45.120
That will be a useful exercise for other reasons
as well because, if you recall, we have no

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:49.390
equality and diversity data on transferring
staff, which is 89% of the staff that we have,

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:54.680
so it will be a good opportunity to get the
equality and diversity data completely up

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:55.680
to date.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:59.650
Word of thanks
At the risk of repeating some of the things

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:03.130
that Bill said I would like to just end, if
it is okay by saying some 'thank yous' to

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:09.610
people because this has been such a significant
undertaking.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:13.980
I should start off by saying, as much as I
have said all that I am very conscious that

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:18.230
for a very small number of staff there were
some problems with their pay and I want to

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:19.279
say I am sorry to them.

00:16:19.279 --> 00:16:24.250
They have, by and large, been very patient
and very tolerant and I hope, in the vast

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.700
majority of cases we have sorted problems
out very quickly and often on the same day,

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:33.770
so I am grateful to people for their tolerance.

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:38.300
On behalf of Paul and I who are joint SROs
on the payroll project I think we should say

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.100
really sincere thanks to people who worked
on it.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:45.050
I do not think I have ever seen a group of
people work as hard as the people have who

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:47.110
have done this.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:53.860
I extend that to the NHS Business Services
Authority who have been fantastic I have to

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:56.200
say, and to Logica McKesson, our payroll providers.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:01.390
Finally, just to say thank you to the many
staff actually who have sent in positive feedback

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.540
to both the HR and the finance teams.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:06.200
It has been really appreciated.

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:09.400
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Jo.

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:11.530
Jo-Ann Wass: Thank you.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.130
Malcolm Grant: Naquib?

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:19.790
Naquib Kheraj: I just wanted to say we have
ended up with about 800 more people than we

00:17:19.790 --> 00:17:25.920
expected to have in the organisation, and
I think maybe when you are through this hump

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:33.610
we can look at where those extra positions
have come from and just confirm that our funding

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:36.050
enables us to be able to cover them.

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:37.050
Jo-Ann Wass: Thank you.

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:38.050
We absolutely will.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:43.300
We will pick it up as part of the tidying
data refresh stuff that we are doing.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:48.720
I have to say that they are mostly in the
lift and shift category rather; the core structure

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:53.060
has not changed but Paul and I have plans
to pick up that.

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:55.860
Malcolm Grant: And the funding comes with
them.

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.150
Jo-Ann Wass: Yes.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:03.740
Malcolm Grant: Jo, on behalf of everybody
can we convey thanks because when you talk

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:09.130
about people working the midnight oil we really
do know what people have been going through.

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:13.490
There has been a huge investment in trying
to ensure that we got off on the right foot

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:17.650
with the staff who we wanted to appoint and
who we value.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:22.940
We do appreciate the work that has gone into
this and I will, if you are happy, write on

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:28.810
behalf of the Board to convey formally the
Board's appreciation of what has been undertaken.

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:33.730
I do recall a year ago somebody said that
the NHS reforms were so big you could see

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:38.860
them from outer space, which I thought was
a typical understatement.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:45.390
The vignette that we have had this morning
just captures, does it not, the sheer complexity

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:50.390
affecting people's lives, people's employment,
coming to a completely new employer, a complete

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:53.450
uncertainty as to what they would find when
they got here.

00:18:53.450 --> 00:19:00.450
I think the work that Jo's team has done and
Tim's team have done, and Bill, has just managed

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:04.480
to manage a transfer that could otherwise
have been quite disastrous.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:07.290
I think we owe them a very deep debt of thanks.

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.220
So thank you, Jo, for that report.

